    "snippet": "---\ntitle: @oneweekoneband on Tumblr\nauthor: Oneweekoneband\ndate: 2015-10-26\n---\nJon Walker and Ryan Ross, from a 2009 interview in Alternative Press\n\nTo address a kind of elephant in the room: I consider pre-split and post-split Panic different bands. It has long been my firm opinion that the right thing to do when the band agreed to split would have been for Brendon and Spencer to continue to work under a different name, as Ryan and Jon did, and to have quit playing material that was primarily written by Ryan, especially the songs that deal with his father. I went to a Panic show in 2011 and the entire time they performed \"Camisado,\" I kept thinking why? Why would you play this song? What are you doing?\n\nJon later retracted his statement, and has continually expressed that he no loner harbors animosity toward the remaining members of Panic, even expressing interest in playing a reunion show.\n\nWhile his last statement pictured in the excerpt above is a little harsh–and I would agree, somewhat disrespectful–I don't think the base of Jon's argument is wrong. Ryan, later with Jon, did the vast majority of the songwriting on Fever and Pretty. Odd., with the former particularly personal for Ryan. Of course I danced to \"Camisado\" when Brendon, Ian, Dallon, and Spencer were playing it, but it left a sour taste in my mouth.\n\nI mean, candidly–where do you get off playing a song about your ex-bandmate's dead alcoholic abusive father when you've admitted that the two of you no longer talk? What, precisely, entitles you to those words–not legally, but ethically? That's not to say that Brendon was not a founding member of Panic or not vital to their success or not a hardworking, talented musician, but Fever and Pretty. Odd.were both primarily Ryan's brainchild, and it rubs me (and clearly Ryan and Jon) the wrong way to continue working under that name and playing that material.\n\nSo: to my mind, pre-split and post-split Panic are different bands, with different artistic aims that don't mesh well with each other. Throughout the week, I'll refer to them as such, because with such a jarring difference between the two eras, it becomes impossible to make encompassing statements about the aims, successes, and failures of \"P"
