    "snippet": "---\ntitle: '90s, 2000s rock band to release first new album in 8 years\nauthor: Geoff Herbert; Gherbert Com\ndate: 2024-01-08\n---\nA popular rock band is set to release their first new album in nearly a decade.\n\nIncubus singer Brandon Boyd told Flux FM that the band finished recently finished the album and plan to release it this year. It will be Incubus' first full-length album of new material since \"8″ in 2017.\n\n\"We actually finished the album, I guess about, a little over two months ago. And it's like mastered, it's mixed, it's just ready to go,\" Boyd said. \"The sad part about finishing the album in this day and age is that, as much as I would like to just put it out; I would love to put it out tomorrow, and we could put it out tomorrow, but it would get just lost in the shuffle of the internet, you know what I mean?\"\n\nAccording to NME, Boyd expects the first single will be released \"in the next few months\" and the album will likely follow in October. A title and other details were not announced.\n\n\"I think it's a really great record, and I'm really proud of it, and all of us in the band are really proud of it. We had a wonderful time recording it,\" Boyd told Flux FM.\n\nIn addition to being Incubus' first new album in eight years, it will be the band's first new music with bassist Nicole Row. NME reports former bass player Ben Kenney left the group in 2023 after undergoing surgery for a brain tumor, which left him permanently deaf in one year and partially paralyzed in his face.\n\nIncubus, who performed at the Syracuse amphitheater in 2022, is an alternative rock/funk-metal group best known for '90s and 2000s hits like \"Drive,\" \"Wish You Were Here,\" \"Stellar,\" \"Nice to Know You,\" \"Anna Molly\" and \"Pardon Me.\" The band re-recorded the 2001 album \"Morning View\" with Row and released it last year.\n\nIncubus is performing \"Morning View\" in its entirety and more hits on its 2025 tour dates, which include concerts June 28 in Uncasville, Connecticut, and June 30"
  },
