    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Oscar winner Gene Hackman, wife Betsy Arakawa found dead in their home\nauthor: David Cifarelli; Dcifarelli Com\ndate: 2024-01-08\n---\nOscar-winning actor Gene Hackman and his wife, classical pianist Betsy Arakawa, were found dead in the couple's home Wednesday, Feb. 26.\n\nHackman, 95, and Arakawa, 63, were found dead along with the couple's dog in their home outside Santa Fe, New Mexico, The Santa Fe New Mexican reported.\n\nThere was no immediate signs of foul play. A cause of death has yet to be revealed.\n\nArakawa and Hackman were married since 1991. Hackman had lived in Santa Fe since the 1980s, according to The Santa Fe New Mexican.\n\nBorn on Jan. 30, 1930 in San Bernardino, California, Hackman's career in film and TV spanned 43 years.\n\nHis early career on Broadway in the 1960s included roles in the shows \"Children from Their Games,\" \"A Rainy Day in Newark,\" \"Any Wednesday,\" \"Poor Richard\" and \"The Natural Look.\"\n\nThe actor, who was also a Marine Corps veteran, had previously been nominated for best supporting actor for starring as Buck Barrow in \"Bonnie and Clyde\" in 1967. He was also nominated for 1970's \"I Never Sang for My Father\" and 1988's \"Mississippi Burning.\"\n\nHackman won his first Academy Award for best actor for \"The French Connection\" in 1972. The actor played Det. Jimmy \"Popeye\" Doyle in the William Friedkin film, which won best picture.\n\nHackman's other major roles in the '70s and '80s included Harry Caul in \"The Conversation\" (1974), Blindman in \"Young Frankenstein\" (1974), Harry Moseby in \"Night Moves\" (1975), Norman Dale in \"Hoosiers\" (1986), and the supervillain Lex Luthor in \"Superman\" (1978), \"Superman II\" (1980) and \"Superman IV: The Quest for Peace\" (1987).\n\nAmong his '90s credits"
