    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Every couple nominated for Oscars in the same year (and who took home the gold statuette)\nauthor: Sydney Bucksbaum\ndate: 2025-03-02\n---\nCall it a workplace event, but the Academy Awards is inherently a romantic spot for date night: You get dressed up in your finest, out of the house, and served free-flowing drinks. And while many nominees bring their significant other as their plus one to the ceremony — and often single them out in their acceptance speeches — it's an even greater relationship flex when both partners are in the race for either the same or separate projects.\n\nFrom the first couple to score acting nods in 1932 to today's lovebirds sharing the same ballot, here's a rundown of the couples who've earned Academy Award nominations in the same year.\n\n1932: Alfred Lunt and Lynn Fontanne\n\nAlfred Lunt and Lynn Fontanne made history as the first couple to be nominated for an Oscar in the same year. After sharing the screen in The Guardsman, they were nominated for Best Actor and Best Actress. —James Mercadante\n\n1940: Laurence Olivier and Vivien Leigh\n\nVivien Leigh won the Oscar for Best Actress for her portrayal of Scarlett O'Hara in Gone With the Wind while her soon-to-be-husband, Laurence Olivier, was nominated for Best Actor in Wuthering Heights. —Sydney Bucksbaum\n\n1967: Richard Burton and Elizabeth Taylor\n\nMarried couple Elizabeth Taylor and Richard Burton were both nominated for their leading roles in Who's Afraid of Virginia Woolf? Though Richard Burton lost, Elizabeth Taylor won. —S.B.\n\n1968: Spencer Tracy and Katharine Hepburn\n\nKatharine Hepburn won the Best Actress Oscar for her role in Guess Who's Coming to Dinner. But her costar, Spencer Tracy — who died 10 months earlier and with whom she had a 25-year affair — was nominated for Best Actor and did not win posthumously. —S.B.\n\n1969: Paul Newman and Joanne Woodward\n\nMarried couple Paul Newman and Joanne Woodward were both nominated for their film Rachel, Rachel, in which she starred and he produced (and directed, though he only got a nomination that year for Best Picture). —S.B.\n\n1975: John Cassavetes and Gena Rowlands\n\nFrequent collaborators and married couple John Cassavetes and"
