    "snippet": "---\ntitle: 40 Hollywood dads and their famous kids, from Dennis and Jack Quaid to Lenny and Zoë Kravitz\nauthor: Maureen Lee Lenker\ndate: 2025-03-21\n---\nYou can't spell \"fame\" without fam! Hollywood is full of hardworking dads, but what about the ones who've raised the next generation of stars? These famous fathers not only open doors to an industry that's notoriously tough to break into but often pass down their creative genes. While some \"nepo babies\" acknowledge that their parent's connections are a major key to their success, others argue that they have to work twice as hard to make appellations for themselves in their own right.\n\nFrom Clint Eastwood's actor son Scott Eastwood to J.J. Abrams' pop star daughter Gracie Abrams, here's a round-up of some of Tinseltown's most memorable father-child pairs.\n\nDennis and Jack Quaid\n\nDespite having a stacked filmography including The Parent Trap (1998), The Day After Tomorrow (2004), and Reagan (2024), Dennis Quaid is confident his son with ex-wife Meg Ryan, Jack, is \"going to eclipse both of us.\"\n\nJack made his onscreen debut as District 1 tribute Marvel in The Hunger Games (2012), and has since racked up an impressive résumé, with acting credits for The Boys, Scream (2022), and Oppenheimer (2023), among others. —James Mercadante\n\nDenzel, John David, and Malcolm Washington\n\nDenzel Washington is a modern acting legend, but his son, John David, is quickly following in his dad's footsteps with leading roles in projects like Spike Lee's BlacKkKlansman (2018), Christopher Nolan's Tenet (2020), and Gareth Edwards' The Creator (2023). In 2024, he starred alongside Samuel L. Jackson in The Piano Lesson — his brother Malcolm's directorial debut, which was produced by Denzel.\n\nJohn David got his start in small uncredited roles in some of Denzel's most beloved films like Malcolm X (1992) and Devil in a Blue Dress (1995), while Malcolm got his start working as a production assistant for Jon Favreau's Chef (2014) and Lee's She's Gotta"
