    "snippet": "---\ntitle: More Americans than ever plan to retire at 62 - but are they at risk of outliving their 401(K)?\nauthor: HELENA KELLY ASSISTANT CONSUMER EDITOR\ndate: 2024-05-13\n---\nMore Americans than ever plan to retire at 62 - but are they at risk of outliving their 401(K)?\n\n- Only 46 percent of Americans plan to work past 62, new data shows\n\n- Trend is being fueled by a 'you only live once attitude' inspired by the pandemic\n\n- READ MORE: Finance guru Dave Ramsey explains the key to early retirement\n\nMore than half of Americans plan to give up work before 62 thanks to a 'you only live once' attitude inspired by the pandemic.\n\nResearch by the New York Federal Reserve shows the age that workers plan to retire has plunged since March 2020.\n\nToday, only 46 percent of Americans under 62 say they expect to work past this age - despite it being three years younger than the traditional retirement age of 65.\n\nIn the six years leading up to the pandemic, that figure averaged 55 percent - and have steadily fallen in the four years since.\n\nThe trend appears to be driven by a 'cultural shift characterized by a rethinking of the value of work,' as well as a booming stock market which has given households more confidence in their financial health, the Fed said in a research note.\n\nMore than half of Americans plan to give up work before 62 thanks to a 'you only live once' attitude inspired by the pandemic\n\nBut whether Americans can actually afford to retire so early is a separate issue. The Treasury warned last week that funding for Social Security will run short by 2035.\n\nAnd the data only shows when Americans intend to retire - rather than when they are actually able to do so in reality.\n\nBut a recent survey by the Employee Benefit Research Institute found the median age at which retirees gave up work was 62. Less than a third of today's retirees worked until 65 or later.\n\nWorkers' 401(K)s have benefited from a buoyant stock market in the past year.\n\nOn February 23, the Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA) hit an all-time high.\n\nIn the same month, the S&P 500 crossed the 5,000 mark for the first time in intraday trading.\n\n"
