    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Firm Timeline\ndate: 2025-01-01\n---\nFirm Timeline\n\n1940s 1950s 1970s 1980s 1990s 2000s 2010s 2020s\n\n-\n\n1946\n\nLegacy firm Berlack Israels & Liberman is founded in New York City by three distinguished corporate and securities lawyers – Harris Berlack, Carlos Israels and James Liberman – who encountered career advancement barriers for Jews at other firms.\n\n-\n\n1948\n\nRome Brown Rudnick & Freed is founded in Boston by four prominent lawyers – Charles Rome, Matthew Brown, Alford Rudnick and Hirsh Freed – who were not welcomed into the partnerships of other leading firms because they were Jewish. Rome leaves the firm in 1949, when he is appointed a justice of the Massachusetts Superior Court.\n\n-\n\n1958\n\nBrown Rudnick & Freed merges with Gesmer & Geller, another Boston firm founded in 1952, and changes its name to Brown Rudnick Freed & Gesmer. The combined entity develops a broad array of commercial and personal legal services, all the while continuing its close connection to government affairs.\n\n-\n\n1975\n\nLegacy firm Alvarado Rus is founded in Southern California by Ray Alvardo, one of Orange County's first Latino lawyers, and Ron Rus, a highly respected business litigation lawyer.\n\n-\n\n1978\n\nAndré Jasse, a corporate and bank regulatory lawyer, becomes Brown Rudnick Freed & Gesmer's first administrative partner. He served in that role, and then as managing partner, for 25 years, leading the firm through an expansion in New England and to Europe.\n\n-\n\n1979\n\nAn accident at the Three Mile Island nuclear power station in Pennsylvania becomes the most significant matter in Berlack Israels & Liberman's history. Prior to and after joining the firm, James Liberman served as general counsel to General Public Utilities, a public holding company for Three Mile Island plant owner Metropolitan Edison. Liberman had chief responsibility for all legal issues emanating from the accident. Over the next 15 years, the case went to