    "docid": "88594",
    "score": 0.5002318620681763,
    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Retired Professors: How old we were you when you retired, did you stay active in the field?\ndate: 2025-06-09\n---\nThis sub is for discussions amongst college & university faculty. Whether you are an adjunct, a lecturer, a grad TA or tenured stream if you teach students at the college level, this space is for you! While we welcome students and non-academics lurking and learning, posts and comments are not allowed. If you're new here, please familiarize yourself with the sub rules and follow them. If you're ever unsure, feel free to reach out to the moderators for clarification.\n\nRetired Professors: How old we were you when you retired, did you stay active in the field?\n\nFor those of you who have made it out, how old were you when you retired and did you remain active with research, writing, teaching as an adjunct or any thing else?\n\nWe have someone at our college who retired at 80 plus. Came back to do special projects in their department.\n\nOthers have left, and returned to adjunct or work in admin. Many were in there 70s who did this.\n\nBut a friend recently retired at 64, thought he would adjunct but now says he wants nothing to do with academia. Totally burn out.\n\nI have a couple years to go. God willing, I'll be in my early 60s. Initially I told our retirement planner that I would probably make a few thousand a year teaching a course or two. But at our next meeting I'm telling them to scrap that as I don't think I'll have the desire to do so and don't want to have to do so.\n\nSo those of you lucky enough to make it out? How old were you and did you cut the strings or stay involved?\n\n(I realize that the retirement generation seems to be largely those who were fortunate enough to land under a pension system and many don't see the ability to retire and I'm sorry for that. So this question is geared towards those who have already gotten out)."
  },
