    "snippet": "---\ntitle: The 40 books I read in 2023\nauthor: Ben Keene\ndate: 2023-12-28\n---\nThe 40 books I read in 2023\n\n…and a 1 line review of each.\n\nHere is a run down of what I read in 2023. It equates to about 40 minutes / 30 pages a day.\n\nThanks to Rebel Book Club for the accountability and inspiration as always!\n\nJoin in and accelerate you or your team's reading habits and community of curious minds with us.\n\nThe scores are the personal impact and enjoyment I got out of each book specifically, not necessarily the topic or RBC conversations.\n\nThe Books\n\n- Bewilderment by Richard Powers: Kicked off 2023 with a novel which reads like a fictional parallel to 'Our House Is On Fire' (the story of the Greta Thunberg's family). Strong nature and relationship writing that gathered pace through the book to a dramatic, poignant climax. 7/10\n\n- A Promised Land by Barack Obama: At 700 pages, it's a beautiful beast. Respect to those that fight everyday to make things better. Obama's rise to power story is truly remarkable. Man can write too. 9/10\n\n- Four Thousand Weeks (RBC Jan) by Oliver Burkeman: Time management for mortals. Kicks hustle culture and productivity hacking into touch and zooms in on the bigger question of our relationship with time. The shortness of the average human lifespan in the UK (80 yrs or 4000 weeks) is not a cause for despair, but relief. You get to choose how best to spend it. 8/10\n\n- We Are Bellingcat (RBC Feb) by Eliot Higgins: From solving the Salisbury poisonings to war crimes in Syria, a group of online investigators appear to be beating the world's most powerful Government intelligence agencies at their own game. When we spoke to Bellingcat's founder, Eliot Higgins, we were impressed to hear what he (and other Bellingcats) are willing to sacrifice considering the massive impact of their digital detective work. We tested out a few of the Bellingcat open source tools at a club quiz with a difference — discovering not only that our own emails are subject to regular hacks but that geo-locating abstract pictures or fact-checking political speeches at speed,"
