    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Jibanananda Das - Wikipedia\nauthor: Authority control databases\ndate: 2005-03-23\n---\nname: Jibanananda Das\nbirth_name: Jibanananda Das\nbirth_date: 17 02 1899\nbirth_place: Barisal, Bengal, British India\ndeath_date: 22 October 1954\ndeath_place: Calcutta, West Bengal, India\noccupation: Poet, writer, and professor\nlanguage: Bengali\nnationality: British Indian (1899–1947)Indian (1947–1954)\nalma_mater: Brajamohan CollegeUniversity of Calcutta\ngenre: Poetry, novels, short stories, criticism\nmovement: Bengali Modernism\nnotableworks: Banalata Sen, Rupasi Bangla, Akashlina, Banalata Sen, Campe, Bodh\nspouse: Labanyaprabha Das ( Gupta)\nchildren: 2\nrelatives: Kusumkumari Das (mother)\nawards: Nikhil Banga Rabindra Sahitya Sammelan Award (1952)Sahitya Akademi Award (1955)\nsignature: Jibanananda Das signature.svg\n\nJibanananda Das (17 February 1899 – 22 October 1954) was a Bengali poet, writer, novelist and essayist in the Bengali language. Popularly called \"Rupashi Banglar Kabi'' ('Poet of Beautiful Bengal'), Das is the most read Bengali poet after Rabindranath Tagore and Kazi Nazrul Islam in Bengal. While not particularly well recognised during his lifetime, today Das is acknowledged as one of the greatest poets in the Bengali language.\n\nBorn in Barisal to a Bengali Hindu family of Baidya caste, Das studied English literature at Presidency College, Kolkata and earned his MA from Calcutta University. He had a troubling career and suffered financial hardship throughout his life. He taught at many colleges but was never granted tenure. He settled in Kolkata after the partition of India. Das died on 22 October 1954, eight days after being hit by a tramcar. Witnesses said that though the tramcar had blown its whistle, Das did not stop, and got struck."
