    "snippet": "---\ntitle: List of anime distributed in the United States\ndate: 2021-03-10\n---\nTemplate:Short description\n\nTemplate:Article for deletion/dated\n\nThese anime series have been shown and have achieved varying levels of popularity in the United States and Canada, this is contributed to the era known as the \"anime boom\" which lasted from the mid-1990s to mid-2000s. In the United States, most anime can be seen televised on channels, with channels such as Adult Swim's Toonami block, and Toku airing anime targeted toward young adults to adult audiences, and with channels such as Disney XD, and Nicktoons airing anime targeted toward children audiences. Anime can also be viewed online legally on streaming websites such as Anime Network, Funimation's FunimationNow platform, Crunchyroll, Netflix, Crackle, YouTube, HBO Max and Hulu.\n\n1960s[]\n\nIn the 1960s, Astro Boy, Speed Racer and Kimba the White Lion were introduced to the US and were received positively. However audiences didn't see them as Japanese, possibly because they resembled most American cartoons of the time. If you were an anime fan in America at the time you would have to rely on fansubs.[1]\n\n- Films\n\n| Name | Licensor | Release Date |\n\n|---|---|---|\n\n| The Tale of the White Serpent | Global Pictures | |\n\n| Magic Boy | Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer | |\n\n| Alakazam the Great | American International | |\n\n| Gulliver's Travels Beyond the Moon | Continental Distributing Inc. | |\n\n| Jack and the Witch | American International | |\n\n| The Little Norse Prince | American International |\n\n- TV series\n\n| Name | Licensor | Network | Start date | End date |\n\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n\n| Astro Boy | NBC Enterprises | NBC | ||\n\n| Gigantor | Trans-Lux Television | Syndication | ||\n\n| 8th Man | ABC Films | Syndication | ||\n\n| Kimba the White Lion | NBC Enterprises | NBC | ||\n\n| Marine Boy | Seven Arts Television | Syndication | ||\n\n| Princess Knight | Right Stuf Inc. | Unknown | ||