    "snippet": "---\ntitle: DualShockers\nauthor: Dev Tugnait\ndate: 2023-12-06\n---\nSummary\n\n- Some anime series, like Deadman Wonderland and Btooom!, end before their manga counterparts due to low popularity and insufficient source material.\n\n- Studio bankruptcy, like in the case of Gangsta., can lead to an anime ending prematurely and not fully covering the manga's story.\n\n- The pacing differences and ongoing manga can also result in an anime like Fairy Tail and Fruits Basket ending before the manga, leaving certain plot developments and character arcs unadapted.\n\nAnime series often have a complex relationship with their source material, known as manga. While many anime faithfully adapt the entire storyline of the manga, a notable list of series concluded before their manga counterpart.\n\n15 Best Anime With Under 100 Episodes, Ranked\n\nExplore some of the best short anime like Death Note and Made in Abyss, which feature less than 100 episodes.\n\nThese anime, like Hunter x Hunter, Trigun, and Berserk, often feature unique endings or deviate from the manga midway due to various reasons such as budget constraints, scheduling issues, or creative choices. Some are given a second chance to adapt the complete story, while others leave fans longing for a resolution. We have compiled a list of the most popular anime that ended before the manga.\n\nUpdated by Dev Tugnait on December 3, 2023: We have updated the list with five new entries. This intriguing selection continues to captivate fans with tales where the anime adaptations concluded before their manga counterparts. Dive in to rediscover these series and explore the differences between their anime and manga versions!\n\n15 Deadman Wonderland\n\nMy Anime List (MAL) Score - 7.15\n\nWhy Did Deadman Wonderland End?\n\n- Anime wasn't popular among fans\n\n- The series ended after 12 episodes\n\nDeadman Wonderland, an anime adaptation of the manga by Jinsei Kataoka and Kazuma Kondou, aired in 2011 but ended after just 12 episodes. The anime's conclusion left out a major portion of the manga's storyline, primarily because the anime didn't garner sufficient popularity and ratings to justify continuation.\n\nThe manga was also ongoing when the anime concluded, which meant limited source material. Key elements and story arcs were left out, including the backstory of key characters like Shiro and Ganta, the revelation about Ganta's Red Man identity, and the entire"
