    "snippet": "---\ntitle: 30 Young Adult Books Featuring Dance and Dancers\ndate: 2021-11-05\n---\nWhether they're about writing, baking, running, drawing, or something else entirely, I love to read books that feature a protagonist who's passionate about something. There are few moments we're able to shine brighter than when we pursue what we love most, and I think it's incredibly important that we encourage this passion in teens; to embrace what makes them happy and find joy in it, even if purely for the value inherent in creativity and self-expression.\n\nOne passion I especially admire in others is the ability to dance. While I'm not particularly coordinated or graceful, I'm always inspired and deeply moved when I see what dancers are able to convey through movement. The emotions and stories they're able to tell through a leap, a fall, or a carefully placed appendage are no less powerful than what a writer is able to express through the writing of a book, and their dedication to their craft no less admirable. Today's post, that explores 30 young adult books that feature dance and/or dancers, combines the best of both worlds!\n\nIf you enjoyed today's post and found it helpful, please consider checking out Pop! Goes The Reader's Patreon! In addition to exclusive rewards like an extensive edition of Hot Off The Press that details over 300+ new middle grade, young adult and adult books being published each month as well as digital downloads for your desktop and devices, I also share curated book lists like this one on a specific theme or subject every month. Membership on Patreon makes a real, tangible difference in helping to support Pop! Goes The Reader and allowing me to do what I love and makes posts like this one, that are accessible to everyone, possible. Thank you!\n\nPublication Date March 1st 2022 by Simon & Schuster Books for Young Readers\n\nGoodreads ● Amazon ● Chapters ● The Book Depository ● Barnes & Noble ● IndieBound\n\nIn this raw, searingly honest debut young adult novel, a former aspiring ballerina must confront her past in order to move forward from a devastating fall that leaves her without the use of her legs.\n\nGenie used to fouetté across the stage. Now the only thing she's turning are the wheels to her wheelchair. Genie was the star pupil at her exclusive New York dance school, with a bright future and endless possibilities before her. Now that the future she's spent years building toward has"
