    "snippet": "---\ntitle: How Many Blogs Exist in 2025, and Do Bloggers Still Make Money?\nauthor: Harsha Kiran\ndate: 2025-01-01\n---\nBlogs have a far greater influence than many people realize. In 2025, blogging continues to be a powerful tool for brands and businesses, with 77% of internet users regularly reading blogs. In fact, studies show that 9 out of 10 consumers make purchasing decisions influenced by blog content.\n\nBlogging isn't just a content strategy — it's a vital marketing channel for businesses to build brand authority, engage audiences, and drive conversions.\n\nWell-crafted blog posts help introduce products, answer customer questions, and leave lasting impressions on readers.\n\nStaying updated on blogging trends is essential for creating content that resonates. The latest blogging statistics offer valuable insights for marketers, bloggers, and businesses looking to grow their online presence and income streams.\n\nRead on to discover the most recent blogging trends and stats you need to know in 2025.\n\nEditor's Choice\n\n- Approximately 7.5 million blog posts are published daily across various platforms.\n\n- There are over 600 million blogs online in 2025.\n\n- WordPress users publish around 70 million posts per month, averaging about 2.33 million posts daily.\n\n- Approximately 83% of internet users, equating to about 4.44 billion people, read blog posts regularly.\n\n- Around 37% of blog readers are aged between 41-60 years.\n\n- 42.8% of high-traffic blogs (receiving over 50,000 sessions per month) are in the food niche.\n\n- The most popular blog niches are lifestyle (11.4%), personal development (9.6%), and entrepreneurship (7.9%).\n\n- Publishing 1–2 blog posts per week is considered optimal for maintaining quality and consistency.\n\n- Only 3% of bloggers opt for a daily publishing schedule.\n\nGeneral Blog Statistics\n\nThere are over 600 million blogs on the internet as of May 2023. Out of 1.9 billion websites, it is more than a third of all websites worldwide.\n\nThis number only shows that blogging is still relevant in this age. As this continues to grow, competition on blogs will also increase.\n\nHere's the total number of blogs online and other blog-related statistics:\n\nNumber of Blogs Worldwide"
