    "snippet": "---\ntitle: LOVERS, ENEMIES AND OTHER DEDICATEES\nauthor: Fran R Schumer\ndate: 2025-01-30\n---\nSupported by\n\nLOVERS, ENEMIES AND OTHER DEDICATEES\n\nWRITING a book is often sheer pain and drudgery, but writing the dedication is a moment most authors savor, a chance to pay back old debts, even the score or give one's spouse (or former spouse) a reward for putting up with all those nights of writers' angst. Dedications are sometimes as original, humorous and witty as the books they precede. And a few are as banal.\n\nTo whom do authors dedicate books? Everyone. Ann Beattie dedicated her novel ''Love Always'' to her former psychotherapist, John Baer Train. James Thurber and E. B. White dedicated ''Is Sex Necessary?'' to ''Daisy and Jeanie,'' two of Thurber's dogs. ''They were the first two female names that came to mind,'' White explained apologetically in Burton Bernstein's ''Thurber: A Biography.''\n\nThe more prolific the author, the more creative he must be in dreaming up dedicatees. Isaac Asimov, now working on book No. 365, dedicated No. 247 (''Laughing Space'') to the sense of humor and No. 318 (''Norby and the Lost Princess'') to Mohonk Mountain House in upstate New York, Bermuda and Manhattan - three of his favorite places.\n\nMost authors stick to the customary order -parents first, then spouses and editors. Probably the nicest dedication to a parent is Sherwood Anderson's in ''Winesburg, Ohio'': ''To the memory of my mother, Emma Smith Anderson, whose keen observations on the life about her first awoke in me the hunger to see beneath the surface of lives, this book is dedicated.'' DEDICATIONS to spouses range from the no-frills - ''For Roger'' (Bobbie Ann Mason's ''Shiloh and Other Stories'') - to the playful - ''For my ouaffe, from her oosban'' (J. Bryan 3d's ''Hodgepodge: A Commonplace Book,'' to be published this fall - ''My wife is French,'' he explained recently). Ernest Hemingway dedicated books to his wives (that took up four). If an author is going"
