    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Hollis's Hospital\ndate: 2020-04-01\n---\nHistory\n\nGet started by choosing a section below. The content for that section will then appear below the buttons.\n\nSome notable descendants of the Founder\n\nThomas Hollis II the founder had three sons Thomas III, Nathaniel and John. Each worked in the family firm – at least for a time. All three became, like their father, involved with the Worshipful Company of Drapers, with two of them becoming Liverymen, Wardens and in Thomas III, the eldest son's case, reaching the pinnacle of the Company, the Master.\n\nSons, grandsons and great grandsons were also Drapers and all served as Trustees of Hollis's Hospital.\n\nHere are some short thumbnail sketches including one or two grandsons and indeed one great grandson, the extraordinary Thomas V.\n\nHollis's Hospital is very lucky to have two well written and fascinating pieces on two of the most notable of the founder's descendants, Thomases III and V. These articles, one on Thomas III by the Reverend Professor Peter J Gomes of Harvard University and the other on Thomas V by the expert and eminent art historian Dr David Wilson FSA have been kindly donated. Please click on the separate tabs to read these two extraordinary and brilliant biographies.\n\nThomas Hollis III – 'The Most Bountiful Benefactor'\n\nThe eldest son of the founder, Thomas III was, just like his father, an industrious and successful businessman. Continuing his father's business and helping to make it very profitable, Thomas also carried on the family tradition of generous charitable giving. Fluent in French, with good Latin and some Greek and having studied accountancy, he was not embarrassed by making and accumulating wealth, he just wanted to ensure it was put to good use. Harvard University in Cambridge, Massachusetts, became his favoured beneficiary where he was happy to help establish civil and religious liberty. Not only did he provide the university with huge sums of money, he also endowed two Professorships, one in Divinity and another in Mathematics and Natural Philosophy – two of the oldest Chairs at Harvard and indeed North America. The gifts were so useful that the university named one of its Halls after him. He was also a Trustee of the Hollis's Hospital.\n\nPlease follow this link to read a fascinating biography of Thomas III by professor Peter J Gomes of Harvard University, reproduced here by kind permission of the Houghton Library Bulletin, Harvard University. It is titled 'Thomas Holl"
